# The two Android trust snapshots are pinned by SHA-256 in
# `packages/phone_auth_native/android/trust-snapshots.json`, and
# `scripts/update_android_trust_snapshots.py` — which CI runs on every push —
# hashes the file on disk and compares.
#
# Git for Windows defaults to `core.autocrlf=true`, so a plain clone rewrites
# every LF in these files to CRLF on the way out. The blob in the repository
# stays LF, the file on disk does not, and the digest of 943 changed line
# endings is a different digest: the check fails on a clean checkout, for a
# reason that has nothing to do with the trust data. This working tree was in
# exactly that state — `git ls-files --eol` reported `i/lf w/crlf` — and it
# only looked fine because the file had not been checked out since the script
# last wrote it. CI never saw it because CI runs on Linux.
#
# `text eol=lf` rather than `-text`: the bytes are what matter, and they are
# still text worth reading in a diff. What they must never be is
# platform-dependent, because one of the two decides which apps may claim to
# speak for a web origin and the other decides how far a passkey scopes.
packages/phone_auth_native/android/src/main/res/raw/privileged_browsers.json text eol=lf
packages/phone_auth_native/android/src/main/res/raw/public_suffix_list.dat text eol=lf

# Byte-exact by construction: a sealed vault export the restore tests open.
# Git already detects it as binary; saying so means that never depends on
# whether a NUL happens to land in the first 8000 bytes.
mobile/test/fixtures/vault-export-v1.bakv binary
